<h1>Automotive Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Automotive conveyors transport vehicle bodies, components, and assemblies through stamping, body shop welding, paint systems, and final assembly. Conveyor types include overhead power-and-free systems for body-in-white transport, skid conveyors for paint shop processing, slat and chain floor conveyors for final assembly, and pallet transfer systems for component sub-assembly. The shift to electric vehicles adds specialized conveyor requirements for battery module and pack assembly.</p>
<h2 id="body-heading">Body Shop Conveyors</h2>
<p>Body shop conveyors transport vehicle bodies through robotic welding, riveting, and adhesive bonding stations. The conveyor must position the body precisely for robotic access while handling the weight of a partially assembled body-in-white (BIW). Body shop conveyors share floor space with robotic welding cells, so cell guarding follows the industrial robot safety practices summarized in the <a href="https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-4-safety-hazards/chapter-4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA Technical Manual chapter on industrial robots</a>.</p>
<h3>Overhead Power-and-Free</h3>
<p>Power-and-free conveyors are the backbone of body shop transport. The power chain runs continuously on the upper track. Body carriers (trolleys on the free track below) engage and disengage the power chain at stations. This allows individual bodies to accumulate at stations while others continue moving, critical for mixed-model production where different body styles require different weld programs and dwell times.</p>
<p>Power-and-free systems handle vertical and horizontal routing, curves, inclines, and switches. A single system can route bodies through multiple parallel welding cells and converge them back to a single line. Overhead conveyors also need guarding where loads pass over aisles and work areas, as covered by <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/automotive-geely-overhead-carriers.webp" width="1000" height="750" alt="Painted car bodies hang from yellow overhead carriers in a line while workers in blue uniforms work at the rear of the nearest body." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Car bodies hang from overhead carriers on a Geely assembly line in Ningbo, China. Overhead transport leaves the floor clear, so operators can reach the body from all sides. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geely_assembly_line_in_Beilun,_Ningbo.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Siyuwj, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/automotive-daifuku-power-and-free-diagram.webp" width="1000" height="355" alt="Labeled side-view diagram of a power-and-free conveyor showing a chain with power trolleys and pusher dogs on the upper track and carriers on front and rear trolleys on the lower track, with a stopper." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Daifuku's labeled diagram of a power-and-free conveyor. The chain, power trolleys, and pusher dogs run on the upper track. Each carrier hangs from a front and rear trolley on the free track below, and a stopper is shown at the right. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.daifuku.com/solution/automotive/pf/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Daifuku</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Skillet Conveyors</h3>
<p>Skillet conveyors use large, flat platforms (skillets) that carry the vehicle body at floor level through welding and assembly stations. Operators and robots access the body from all sides without overhead obstructions. Friction-driven roller beds or linear motor systems advance skillets. Skillet systems provide flexible routing, skillets can be diverted to parallel stations or bypass stations for model-specific processes.</p>
<h2 id="paint-heading">Paint Shop Conveyors</h2>
<p>Paint shop conveyors transport vehicle bodies through pretreatment (cleaning, phosphate coating), electrocoat (e-coat), primer spray, basecoat spray, clearcoat spray, and oven curing. The conveyor must handle immersion processes (bodies dip into treatment tanks), high-temperature curing ovens, and clean paint booth conditions.</p>
<h3>Skid Conveyor Systems</h3>
<p>Skid conveyors carry bodies on metal skid frames through paint processes. The skid provides a universal mounting interface, different body styles mount on the same skid design using adjustable fixtures. Skid conveyors run on chain-driven or friction-driven roller beds. The skid must withstand repeated paint oven cure cycles without warping or degrading.</p>
<p>In immersion processes (pretreatment and e-coat), the skid conveyor system includes pendulum or rotation mechanisms that tilt or rotate the body as it enters and exits the treatment tank. Rotation ensures complete surface coverage and drainage of treatment chemicals from body cavities. Dürr's <a href="https://www.durr.com/en/products/paint-shop-application-technology/pretreatment-and-electrocoating/rodip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RoDip</a> process rotates the entire body in the tank, with custom-programmable rotation, to optimize dipping, flooding, and draining.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/automotive-durr-rodip-m.webp" width="600" height="400" alt="A bare metal vehicle body stands tilted on end on a rotating carrier inside a stainless steel treatment tank enclosure." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A vehicle body turned on end as it rotates through a treatment tank on Dürr's RoDip M system. Rotating the body lets liquid reach and drain from its cavities. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.durr.com/en/products/paint-shop-application-technology/pretreatment-and-electrocoating/rodip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dürr</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Paint Booth Conveyors</h3>
<p>Conveyors through paint spray booths run at slow, steady speed. Application robots track conveyor motion, so speed variation can show up as uneven film thickness. The conveyor must also run cleanly, because particles cause paint defects. Enclosed drives and low-contamination lubrication are standard practice.</p>
<h2 id="assembly-heading">Final Assembly Conveyors</h2>
<p>Final assembly conveyors move painted bodies through a long sequence of stations where operators install wiring, trim, glass, seats, powertrain, wheels, and fluids. Final assembly is the most labor-intensive shop, conveyor design directly affects operator ergonomics and line balance.</p>
<h3>Slat Conveyors</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/slat-conveyors/">Slat conveyors</a> use interlocking metal or composite slats forming a continuous flat surface. Operators stand on or walk alongside the slat surface while performing assembly tasks on the vehicle. Slat conveyors run slowly in final assembly, slow enough for operators to complete multi-minute tasks at each station.</p>
<h3>Inverted Power-and-Free</h3>
<p>Inverted power-and-free conveyors run on floor-level tracks with the vehicle carrier rising from below. This provides full 360-degree access to the vehicle without overhead obstructions. Operators work at natural standing height. The vehicle can be raised or lowered hydraulically at different stations to optimize ergonomic access, raised for underbody work, lowered for roof and interior work.</p>
<h3>AGVs in Assembly</h3>
<p>Automated Guided Vehicles (AGVs) increasingly replace fixed conveyors in flexible <a href="/industries/assembly-line-conveyors/">assembly operations</a>. Each AGV carries one vehicle body on a platform and follows programmable routes between stations. AGVs enable model-specific routing (different models visit different stations), easy line reconfiguration (no fixed infrastructure to move), and variable pacing (each AGV moves independently). The trade-off is higher per-unit cost and more complex traffic management versus fixed conveyors.</p>
<h2 id="ev-heading">EV Battery Assembly Conveyors</h2>
<p>Electric vehicle battery manufacturing introduces conveyor requirements not found in traditional automotive production. Battery module and pack assembly lines handle heavy components (large battery packs), require electrostatic discharge (ESD) protection, need thermal monitoring integration, and must interface with fire suppression systems.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cell-to-module assembly:</strong> Pallet transfer conveyors carry fixtures holding individual battery cells through stacking, welding (laser or ultrasonic), and testing stations. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Servo indexing</a> provides precise positioning for robotic cell insertion.</li>
<li><strong>Module-to-pack assembly:</strong> Heavy-duty conveyors or AGVs transport battery packs through module insertion, fastening, thermal interface application, and electrical connection stations. Conveyor capacity must handle the full pack weight plus tooling.</li>
<li><strong>ESD protection:</strong> Conveyor surfaces, belts, and pallets dissipate static to protect battery management electronics and sensitive components. ESD-safe belt materials and grounded frames are standard.</li>
<li><strong>Thermal safety:</strong> Thermal sensors integrated with the conveyor system monitor cell and module temperatures. Conveyors include emergency stop zones and interface with the plant fire suppression system in case of a thermal runaway event during assembly. Confirm the suppression method with the facility fire protection engineer.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="component-heading">Component &amp; Sub-Assembly Conveyors</h2>
<p>Tier 1 and Tier 2 automotive suppliers use conveyors for component sub-assembly: dashboard modules, door panels, instrument clusters, exhaust systems, and transmission components. These lines typically use modular <a href="/industries/assembly-line-conveyors/">assembly conveyors</a> such as pallet transfer systems.</p>
<p>Just-in-sequence (JIS) delivery requires sub-assemblies to arrive at the vehicle assembly line in the exact order matching the vehicle build sequence. Conveyor systems at supplier facilities sort and sequence sub-assemblies using <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">diverter conveyors</a> and accumulation zones that buffer and release components in sequence. Barcode or RFID tracking on the conveyor system verifies sequence order before shipping.</p>
<p>For information on conveyor vendors serving automotive applications, see our <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">manufacturer directory</a>. For custom engineering requirements, see our <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/custom/">custom conveyor design guide</a>.</p>
